{
  "_comment": "The person picker across the Mac bridge (docs/design-people.md § UX iteration 3). Vitest (frontend/src/utils/personPickerBridge.test.ts) builds each web_to_native `wire` from `input` with the English strings, and resolves each native_to_web `payload` against its `slot` to `effect`. Swift (BristlenoseTests/PersonPickerContractTests.swift) decodes every `wire` exactly as BridgeHandler receives it and builds every `payload` from `native`. Change a shape here first, then both sides. Version 3 (6 Oct 2026, design-people.md §J8): rows carry their own codes, the new row the next free one, and a reply says which row it came from — a listed name, a typed one (`new`) or That's Me (`me`) — so a typed name is never read as a pick. A reply the SPA refuses resolves to `{\"taken\": name}`. Version 4 (6 Oct 2026): the ✕ — native replies `{\"kind\": \"clear\"}`, carrying no name, and the SPA applies it only where the slot holds a moderator or observer (§J8.8). Version 5 (6 Oct 2026): rename in place — native replies `{\"kind\": \"rename\", \"name\": …}` for the current, confirmed row; the SPA refuses a taken name and treats it as a new spelling for that person everywhere (§J8.16). Version 6 (6 Oct 2026): a recode (§J7 R1) — web sends `roles`, the other roles the speaker can move to, each with its own rows, codes and next code (built from `knownByRole`, the people by role); native replies with `role` when the pick was made under another role, and the SPA resolves it as that person, or someone new, in that role.",
